;; What the reference interpreter's C leg does about each `buf.validate`
;; constraint in `proto/ui/ui_ast.proto` — the input to
;; `renderer-gen.ui-ast-constraints-json`, which publishes
;; `output/manifests/ui-ast-constraints.json` from it.
;;
;; WHY A REGISTRY AT ALL, rather than deriving everything. Two of the four
;; verdicts ARE derived and are not trusted here: `nanopb-size` is recomputed
;; against `ui_ast.options` on every emit and refuses unless the bound is exact,
;; and `renderer-guard` existence-checks both its anchors. What cannot be derived
;; is the JUDGEMENT — that an unenforced constraint is acceptable, and what an
;; illegal value does instead. That is a decision, and this file is where it is
;; on the record instead of in nobody's head.
;;
;; TOTALITY RUNS BOTH WAYS AND IS THE POINT. A constraint with no entry fails the
;; emit, so a new `lte` cannot land unruled-on; an entry naming a constraint the
;; proto no longer declares fails too, so this file can only ratchet. It is NOT a
;; baseline: no entry corresponds to a finding a fix removes, and every entry is
;; a statement about what the C leg does rather than a parked defect.
;;
;; THE FOUR VERDICTS, in one line each — the emitter's docstring is the full
;; definition and what it verifies:
;;   :nanopb-size        `ui_ast.options` carries the exact counterpart; the
;;                       strip removes no enforcement. Recomputed every emit.
;;   :renderer-guard     a hand-written C guard refuses. `:guard` and `:test`
;;                       are existence-checked.
;;   :enforced-elsewhere no guard names it, but a clause somewhere else refuses
;;                       the same input. `:by` names it; `:harm` names the
;;                       residue, usually WHEN it refuses rather than whether.
;;   :unenforced         nothing refuses it. `:harm` says what happens instead.
;;
;; A NOTE ON THE UNENFORCED MAJORITY, so its size is not read as neglect. Most
;; of these are value bounds on COSMETIC scalars whose worst case is a wrong
;; pixel, on a wire whose producer is the party that declared the constraint in
;; the first place. The ones closed are the ones where an illegal value does
;; something a frame cannot show: undefined behaviour in an int32 multiply, a
;; press absorbed from a sibling, a widget or a comparison silently substituted.
;; That line is stated here rather than left implicit, because the honest reason
;; to leave a constraint open is that its harm is bounded and visible — never
;; that closing it looked like work.

{"ui.ArcProps.bg_end_angle"
 {"lte" {:disposition :unenforced
         :rationale "Cosmetic angle on a decorative arc; the producer that declared the bound is the only writer."
         :harm "lv_arc_set_bg_angles subtracts 360 ONCE for a value above it, so 1000 stays 1000 and the arc draws a wrong sweep. No memory hazard."}}

 "ui.ArcProps.bg_start_angle"
